{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"for all \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)","B":"if \\(a\\ne2b\\)","C":"if \\(a\\ne6\\)","D":"if \\(b\\ne0\\)","E":"if \\(b\\ne3\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1960_AMC_12_Q14","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":[],"contentHash":"7e0ee3c766d5919d4a1c87efaf475f7dc27953278030b5bb4f7a0aa8db08888b","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"nonzero-linear-coefficient","label":"Collect the x terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonzero-linear-coefficient":["cnm_042","cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"intersect-two-lines","label":"Compare line slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersect-two-lines":["cnm_h171","cnm_h172"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h171","cnm_h172"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_034","cnm_039"],"id":"classify-the-linear-equation","label":"Check whether the coefficient vanishes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-the-linear-equation":["cnm_042","cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_040","cnm_034","cnm_039"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_039"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":14,"question":"If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers, the equation \\(3x-5+a=bx+1\\) has a unique solution \\(x\\) [the symbol \\(a\\ne0\\) means that \\(a\\) is different from zero]:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Collecting the terms containing \\(x\\) reveals when we can isolate the unknown:\n\\[\n3x-5+a=bx+1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(3-b)x=6-a.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\ne3\\), the coefficient \\(3-b\\) is nonzero, so division gives exactly one solution,\n\\[\nx=\\frac{6-a}{3-b}.\n\\]\nIf \\(b=3\\), we must not divide by \\(3-b\\). Instead, the equation is \\(0=6-a\\). When \\(a=6\\), every real \\(x\\) is a solution; when \\(a\\ne6\\), there is no solution.\n\nTherefore the equation has a unique solution exactly when \\(\\boxed{b\\ne3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_040"],"explanation":"Collecting the terms containing \\(x\\) reveals when we can isolate the unknown:\n\\[\n3x-5+a=bx+1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(3-b)x=6-a.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\ne3\\), the coefficient \\(3-b\\) is nonzero, so division gives exactly one solution,\n\\[\nx=\\frac{6-a}{3-b}.\n\\]\nIf \\(b=3\\), we must not divide by \\(3-b\\). Instead, the equation is \\(0=6-a\\). When \\(a=6\\), every real \\(x\\) is a solution; when \\(a\\ne6\\), there is no solution.\n\nTherefore the equation has a unique solution exactly when \\(\\boxed{b\\ne3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","id":"classify-the-linear-equation","label":"Check whether the coefficient vanishes","legacyRouteId":"classify-the-linear-equation","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1960_AMC_12_Q14.json","sha256":"d1aa0551ffa27940d9383df7ce9470aa7159a0a5ce719050773e77e7d997937c"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1960_AMC_12_Q14#classify-the-linear-equation","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_040"],"id":"nonzero-linear-coefficient","label":"Collect the x terms","legacyRouteId":"nonzero-linear-coefficient","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1960_AMC_12_Q14.json","sha256":"d1aa0551ffa27940d9383df7ce9470aa7159a0a5ce719050773e77e7d997937c"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_scope